<Project>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      MinVer derives the version from the git tag (see Directory.Build.props). Build-time only:
      Directory.Build.props references it with PrivateAssets="all", so it appears in no nuspec.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="MinVer" Version="8.0.0" />
    <!-- Independent JSON Schema validation in the wire-description tests only. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="JsonSchema.Net" Version="9.4.1" />

    <!-- Server requires the matching client patch; DevServer bundles its own Server library. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly" Version="10.0.14" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.DevServer" Version="10.0.14" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.Server" Version="10.0.14" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.FluentUI.AspNetCore.Components" Version="5.0.0" />
    <!--
      A RANGE, NOT A POINT, AND IT IS THE ONE NUMBER CONSUMERS INHERIT. NuGet reads a bare
      `Version="10.0.0"` in a nuspec as ">= 10.0.0" with no ceiling, so the package would resolve
      against EF Core 11. This provider is built on EF internals by design (19 files carry
      `#pragma warning disable EF1001`), and a major EF release moves them, so an unbounded range
      trades a clear restore error for a runtime failure inside IStateManager.

      Npgsql declares the same shape: its 10.0.0 is [10.0.0, 11.0.0) and its 10.0.2 is
      [10.0.4, 11.0.0), the floor raised deliberately once it needed a later patch.

      THE FLOOR IS THE POINT OF THE LOWER BOUND. It is what a consumer is forced onto, so do not
      raise it to chase a patch; raise it only when this provider needs a fix that shipped in one.
      .github/dependabot.yml ignores this family for exactly that reason.

      MICROSOFT.ENTITYFRAMEWORKCORE.RELATIONAL IS THE SECOND NUMBER CONSUMERS INHERIT, and carries
      the same range for the same reason. InfoCarrier.Core has referenced it since R135
      (2026-09-03), and until 2026-09-28 it was a bare `10.0.1` here, so 10.1.x declared it with no
      ceiling beside an EF Core dependency that had one. The other EF packages below are test and
      sample references and reach no nuspec.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="[10.0.1,11.0.0)" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Proxies" Version="10.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory" Version="10.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite" Version="10.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite.NetTopologySuite" Version="10.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer" Version="10.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational" Version="[10.0.1,11.0.0)" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational.Specification.Tests" Version="10.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Specification.Tests" Version="10.0.1" />

    <!--
      ADR-009 Tier C: Firebird, and it is TEST-ONLY. No shipped project references any of these
      four, and none of them may ever appear in src/, because this provider references no backing
      store.

      Firebird is here for what SQLite cannot do at all: table-valued functions, which Firebird
      spells as selectable stored procedures, and the correlated LATERAL join over one. Those are
      the whole of UdfDbFunctionTestBase that Tier B has to leave red.

      THE NATIVE BINARIES COME FROM NUGET AND NOTHING IS INSTALLED. FirebirdSql.Data.FirebirdClient
      carries no `fbclient`, so the engine arrives as two community "native assets" packages that
      drop Firebird 5.0.3 into the output directory, and NativeAssetManager finds the path at run
      time. One database is one file, as SQLite's is; there is no server process, and nothing is
      downloaded during a run. Both platform packages are referenced because CI runs on
      ubuntu-latest and development happens on Windows.

      THE LAST THREE ARE COMMUNITY PACKAGES CARRYING NATIVE CODE, published by one author, and
      that is worth knowing rather than discovering. They run only inside this test project.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Console" Version="10.0.12" />
    <PackageVersion Include="FirebirdSql.EntityFrameworkCore.Firebird" Version="13.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="FirebirdDb.Embedded.NativeAssetManager" Version="1.0.6" />
    <PackageVersion Include="FirebirdDb.Embedded.V5.NativeAssets.Windows.X64" Version="2.0.6" />
    <PackageVersion Include="FirebirdDb.Embedded.V5.NativeAssets.Linux.X64" Version="2.0.6" />
    <!--
      This DependencyInjection entry pins transitive references in tests and samples to 10.0.14,
      the floor required through Blazor's Microsoft.Extensions.Logging dependency. A lower pin
      fails restore with NU1109. Microsoft.Extensions.Http remains a direct product dependency
      at 10.0.12; the shipped projects do not apply this DependencyInjection pin.

      Corrected 2026-11-02: this comment previously said transitive pins never reach a nuspec and
      disabling pinning for packable projects causes NU1109. Central transitive pinning CAN promote
      dependencies into a nuspec. Directory.Build.props enables it for this repository, but both
      shipped projects explicitly disable it. Their restore graphs and packed dependency groups
      must be checked when changing these pins.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" Version="10.0.14" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Http" Version="10.0.12" />
    <!--
      Test-tooling transitive pin. Xml 10.0.14 requires Pkcs >= 10.0.14 on net10.0, so this
      one pin updates both packages past the October 2026 advisories without a separate Pkcs pin.
      Updating the EF specification packages instead would raise the test harness's EF floor.
      Neither cryptography package appears in either shipped project's restore graph.

      Corrected 2026-11-02: this comment previously attributed Xml only to SqlClient in functional
      tests. It arrives through EF specification/design tooling and MSBuild/Roslyn dependencies
      in functional tests and TestUtilities; Pkcs also arrives through SqlClient.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="System.Security.Cryptography.Xml" Version="10.0.14" />

    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="18.10.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="4.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="System.Text.Json" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="coverlet.collector" Version="10.1.0" />

    <!--
      TEST-ONLY, and it buys determinism rather than convenience. `FakeTimeProvider.Advance` fires
      every timer callback that has come due, SYNCHRONOUSLY on the calling thread, which is what
      lets the transaction-eviction tests assert a timeout without sleeping. Measured before it was
      added: one `Advance(50s)` over a 10s period fires six times, and `PeriodicTimer` built over
      the fake releases `WaitForNextTickAsync` on the same call.

      A HAND-ROLLED `TimeProvider` IS NOT A SUBSTITUTE, and the probe that established it is
      worth not repeating. Overriding `GetUtcNow()` alone leaves timers on the SYSTEM clock,
      because that is what the base `TimeProvider.CreateTimer` uses, so a test written against
      one passes only when the real period happens to elapse inside the assertion's wait. That is a
      test green by accident, which is the failure mode this repository treats as a defect.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing" Version="9.10.0" />

    <!--
      TIER D, TEST-ONLY, AND THE STORE ARRIVES IN THE PACKAGE. Mongo2Go embeds the mongod,
      mongoimport and mongoexport binaries for win-x64, linux-x64/arm64 and osx-x64/arm64, so a
      run downloads nothing and installs nothing. That is the same bar Firebird met and the reason
      PostgreSQL did not: EphemeralMongo, the obvious package for this, FETCHES the binaries at
      first use into local app data, which ADR-009 already rejected once.

      MongoDB.EntityFrameworkCore REQUIRES EF Core >= 10.0.11, above this repository's 10.0.1
      floor. That is deliberate rather than tolerated: it makes the test project resolve EF ABOVE
      what src/ compiles against, which is the arrangement a consumer is really in and the one
      that would have caught the 10.1.1 defect months earlier.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Mongo2Go" Version="5.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="MongoDB.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="10.0.4" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
